# Credentials and secrets
.env
*.env
# `*.env` only matches when .env is the FINAL extension, so it misses backups
# like `.env.bak-2026-08-25` or `.env.save`. A timestamped env backup was
# committed to this PUBLIC repo because of that gap. Match any .env* variant,
# then re-allow the example templates.
.env.*
**/.env.*
!.env.example
!.env.*.example

# Runtime data (never commit)
*.db
*.db-shm
*.db-wal
*.log

# OS files
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
*.swo

# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
*.egg-info/
dist/
build/

# IDE
.idea/
.vscode/
*.iml
*.sublime-project
*.sublime-workspace

# Docker
.docker/
docker-compose.override.yml

# Backup files
*.bak
*.backup
*~
hermes-configs/
backups/

# Node
node_modules/
npm-debug.log*

# Temporary files
*.tmp
*.temp
checkpoints/

# Private knowledge bases & runtime artifacts (never commit)
cron/output/
cron/usage_audit.jsonl
cron/ticker_*
# cron/jobs.json is RUNTIME STATE, not source: it carries absolute home paths,
# a Telegram chat id, per-run timestamps, next_run_at claims and failure
# counters. Publishing it leaks PII and hands a new install another machine's
# schedule history. The portable equivalent is cron/README-CRON-SETUP.md,
# which documents every expected job so a fresh Hermes can recreate them.
cron/jobs.json
**/wealthforge*
**/*wealthforge*
**/*ppli-*
**/*estate-tax*
**/Hermes-Vault/

# Private-infra skills (reference personal servers/domains)
**/freshrss-troubleshooting/

# Personal context files & curator backup snapshots (never commit)
**/memories/USER.md
**/memories/MEMORY.md
**/.curator_backups/
# Curator runtime metadata (auto-generated, profile-specific)
**/.curator_state
**/.hub/
**/.usage.json
**/.usage.json.lock
**/auth.lock

# Agent-to-agent NAS exchange (machine-specific, guest-accessible share — never publish)
/mnt/
**/agent-exchange/

# ── Wiki content & graphify artifacts (private knowledge — never commit) ──
# Canonical wiki content lives in ~/.autognosia, outside this repo. These rules
# are preventive: they stop a future agent from committing private knowledge if
# a vault is ever cloned, symlinked, or generated inside the repo tree.
active-wiki/
**/active-wiki/
oracle/brain/
**/oracle/brain/
# Graphify extraction output (regenerable; contains full document text)
graphify-out/
**/graphify-out/
graphify-main-out/
graphify-oracle-out/
**/.graphify_*
**/graph.json
**/graph.html
**/GRAPH_REPORT.md
**/manifest.json

# ── new_stuff/ (duplicate images already published under assets/) ──
# Every README reference points at assets/. new_stuff/ held byte-identical
# copies, so it is untracked to avoid shipping the same images twice.
new_stuff/

# Literal '${HOME}' directory created by an unexpanded shell variable
${HOME}/

# ── Machine-specific profiles (never publish) ──
# These profiles are pinned to hardware only this operator has: a desktop
# LM Studio server on the LAN. They are useless to anyone cloning this repo
# and would leak private network topology. Keep them local-only.
profiles/desktop-researcher/
profiles/desktop-worker/

# ── Machine-specific: operator's home lab (never publish) ──
# Contains specific host inventory, usernames and credentials for one private
# LAN. Useless to anyone else and a standing PII risk.
skills/home-lab-ssh/
profiles/*/skills/home-lab-ssh/

# ── Per-profile skill mirrors ──
# profiles/<name>/skills/ duplicated the top-level skills/ tree (4,100 files).
# Pure duplication that multiplied every PII leak by five. Profiles keep their
# own config.yaml + SOUL.md only.
profiles/*/skills/
profiles/auditor/
profiles/oracle/
profiles/planner/
profiles/researcher/

# Secrets / local state that must never be committed
*.env
.env
*.pem
*.key
credentials.json
token.json
*.sqlite
*.sqlite-wal
*.sqlite-shm

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# NEVER PUBLISH: backups, wiki content, databases, runtime state
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Rationale: these are personal data by nature, not code. They are excluded
# unconditionally -- NOT because a PII scan flagged them, but because they
# have no business in a public repo whether they happen to contain PII today
# or not. A clean scan is not a licence to publish personal data.

# ── Backups (any flavour, any depth) ──
backups/
**/backups/
*.bak
*.bak-*
*.backup
*.orig
*.old
.curator_backups/
**/.curator_backups/
*-backup-*/
archives/
checkpoints/

